---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MedicalDisclaimer from '../components/MedicalDisclaimer.astro';
import Sidebar from '../components/Sidebar.astro';
import PioneerCard from '../components/PioneerCard.astro';
import shinrinYokuImage from '../assets/images/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing/shinrin-yoku-forest-sunlight.jpg';
import vsHikingImage from '../assets/images/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing/forest-bathing-vs-hiking-mindful-standing-still.webp';
import cedarBarkImage from '../assets/images/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing/shinrin-yoku-touching-cedar-bark-phytoncides.webp';
import benchStressImage from '../assets/images/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing/senior-couple-forest-bathing-bench-stress-relief.webp';
import fiveSensesImage from '../assets/images/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing/shinrin-yoku-five-senses-nature-illustration.webp';
import whatToBringImage from '../assets/images/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing/what-to-bring-forest-bathing-checklist-gear.webp';
import accessibleTrailImage from '../assets/images/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing/forest-bathing-accessible-trail-rollator-senior.webp';
import fourSeasonsImage from '../assets/images/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing/shinrin-yoku-forest-path-four-seasons.webp';
import ExploreMethods from '../components/ExploreMethods.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Shinrin-Yoku: The Japanese Forest Bathing Practice That Can Transform Your Health After 50"
  description="Discover Shinrin-Yoku, Japan's science-backed forest bathing practice. Learn how spending mindful time in nature can reduce stress, lower blood pressure, and boost immunity for seniors."
  keywords="shinrin-yoku, forest bathing, senior wellness, nature therapy, stress relief, japanese wellness, forest therapy"
  canonicalUrl="https://simpleseniorfitness.com/shinrin-yoku-forest-bathing"
  author="Simple Senior Fitness"
  datePublished="2025-12-18"
  dateModified="2025-12-18"
>
  <main class="flex-1 w-full max-w-6xl mx-auto pb-12 px-6">
    <section class="pt-10 pb-8 max-w-4xl">
      <span class="inline-block py-1.5 px-3 rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-accent-dark text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-4 border border-emerald-200">Nature Therapy</span>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-[1.2rem] lg:text-[2rem] font-medium leading-tight text-text-main-light mb-6">
        Shinrin-Yoku: The Japanese Forest Bathing Practice That Can Transform Your Health After 50
      </h1>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 border-l-4 border-accent-dark pl-4 py-1">
        <div class="flex flex-col">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-text-main-light">By Simple Senior Fitness</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-text-muted-light">Dec 18, 2025</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-12">
      <article class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-8">

        <!-- Hero Image -->
        <figure class="mb-8">
          <div class="w-full aspect-[16/9] bg-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg relative">
            <img
              src={shinrinYokuImage.src}
              alt="Peaceful forest path with sunlight filtering through trees, perfect for Shinrin-Yoku forest bathing"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-text-muted-light mt-3 italic text-center">
            Shinrin-Yoku, or forest bathing, has been a cornerstone of preventive healthcare in Japan since 1982.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Introduction -->
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          <span class="text-6xl float-left font-serif font-bold text-accent-dark mr-4 mt-[-8px] leading-none">I</span>magine a health practice so powerful that it can lower your blood pressure, reduce stress hormones by 50%, boost your immune system, and improve your mood, all without a single medication or piece of equipment. This isn't science fiction. It's <strong>Shinrin-Yoku</strong>, the Japanese practice of "forest bathing" that has been scientifically proven to offer profound health benefits, especially for people over 50.
        </p>

        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          Shinrin-Yoku (pronounced shee-rin-yo-ku) literally translates to "forest bath" or "absorbing the forest atmosphere." But this isn't about hiking for miles or achieving fitness goals. It's about mindfully immersing yourself in nature, using all five senses to connect with the natural world around you.
        </p>

        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          Since its development in 1982, Shinrin-Yoku has become a cornerstone of preventive healthcare in Japan, where doctors regularly prescribe "forest therapy" to patients. Now, as research continues to validate its remarkable benefits, this ancient practice is gaining recognition across North America as a powerful tool for healthy aging.
        </p>

        <!-- What Is Shinrin-Yoku Section -->
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-3xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light pt-4" id="what-is-shinrin-yoku">
          What Is Shinrin-Yoku? Understanding Forest Bathing
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          Shinrin-Yoku was officially developed as a wellness practice by the Japanese government in 1982 as part of a national health program. The concept was simple but revolutionary: intentional, mindful time spent in forested areas can significantly improve both physical and mental health.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          Unlike hiking or traditional outdoor recreation, Shinrin-Yoku is not about physical exertion or reaching a destination. Instead, it's about slowing down, being present, and consciously engaging with nature through all your senses: seeing the play of light through leaves, hearing birdsong and rustling branches, feeling bark textures and cool breezes, smelling the earthy forest scent, and even tasting fresh air.
        </p>

        <figure class="my-8">
          <div class="w-full aspect-[3/2] rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg">
            <img
              src={vsHikingImage.src}
              alt="Older adult standing still on a woodland path with eyes closed, while a hiker walks briskly past in the background"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-text-muted-light mt-3 italic text-center">
            The difference in one picture: hiking passes through the forest, Shinrin-Yoku stops inside it.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-2xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light pt-2" id="science-behind">
          The Science Behind Forest Bathing
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          What makes Shinrin-Yoku more than just a pleasant walk in the woods is the growing body of scientific research supporting its health benefits. Japanese researchers have conducted numerous studies showing that time spent in forests triggers measurable physiological changes in the human body.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          The secret lies partly in <strong>phytoncides</strong>, natural compounds released by trees and plants. When you breathe in forest air, you inhale these phytoncides, which have been shown to boost the activity of natural killer (NK) cells in your immune system. These cells are crucial for fighting infections and even preventing certain types of cancer.
        </p>

        <figure class="my-8">
          <div class="w-full aspect-[3/2] rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg">
            <img
              src={cedarBarkImage.src}
              alt="Close-up of an older adult hand resting on the furrowed bark of a cedar tree in dappled forest light"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-text-muted-light mt-3 italic text-center">
            Cedars and other conifers release phytoncides, the airborne compounds linked to higher natural killer cell activity.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Comparison Box -->
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">
              Hiking Focuses On:
            </h4>
            <ul class="space-y-2">
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Physical exercise and endurance</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Covering distance or reaching summits</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Achieving fitness goals</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Often involves challenging terrain</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">
              Shinrin-Yoku Focuses On:
            </h4>
            <ul class="space-y-2">
              <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Sensory immersion and mindfulness</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Present-moment awareness</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Therapeutic benefits</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Gentle, accessible movement</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Benefits Section -->
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-3xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light pt-4" id="benefits">
          Powerful Health Benefits of Shinrin-Yoku for Seniors Over 50
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          Research has revealed that Shinrin-Yoku offers specific benefits that are particularly valuable for people over 50. Here's what the science tells us:
        </p>

        <!-- Benefit Cards -->
        <div class="grid gap-6 my-8">
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">1. Support for Healthier Blood Pressure</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">High blood pressure is one of the most common health concerns among adults over 55. Japanese forest medicine research has repeatedly observed that time spent walking slowly in a forest is associated with modest reductions in both systolic and diastolic blood pressure, effects that can linger for days after the visit. For seniors on blood pressure medication, Shinrin-Yoku can be a useful complement to (never a replacement for) medical treatment.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">2. Reduced Stress and Cortisol Levels</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Shinrin-Yoku is widely associated with measurable drops in cortisol, the body's primary stress hormone, compared with time spent in urban environments. Forest medicine researchers have found that even short forest visits (sometimes as brief as 15 minutes) can reduce cortisol, slow the pulse, and ease blood pressure.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">3. Enhanced Immune System Function</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Dr. Qing Li and colleagues at Nippon Medical School have documented that forest bathing is associated with a meaningful boost in natural killer (NK) cell activity, part of the immune system that helps the body fight infection and disease. Their research suggests these effects can persist for several days after a forest visit. For seniors whose immune systems naturally weaken with age, this matters.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">4. Improved Mood and Reduced Depression</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Forest therapy is consistently associated with lower depression and anxiety scores among older adults. The combination of gentle activity, rich sensory input, and a calming natural environment creates a setting that supports emotional wellbeing in a way few indoor interventions can match.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">5. Better Sleep Quality</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Forest bathing reduces stress hormones that interfere with sleep, increases physical tiredness through gentle activity, regulates circadian rhythms through natural light exposure, and promotes relaxation. Studies show significant improvements in both sleep quality and duration.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">6. Cognitive Benefits and Mental Clarity</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Natural environments allow the brain to rest from constant urban stimulation, a process called "attention restoration." This mental break improves focus, creativity, and memory. For seniors concerned about maintaining mental sharpness, Shinrin-Yoku offers a natural cognitive boost.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">7. Reduced Inflammation & Pain Management</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Chronic inflammation contributes to arthritis, heart disease, diabetes, and other long-term conditions. Forest bathing has been linked to lower levels of inflammatory markers and to gentle relief of chronic pain, with benefits that can extend well beyond the day of the walk itself.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- How to Practice Section -->
        <figure class="my-8">
          <div class="w-full aspect-[3/2] rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg">
            <img
              src={benchStressImage.src}
              alt="Senior couple sitting quietly together on a wooden bench in a sunlit forest clearing, shoulders relaxed"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-text-muted-light mt-3 italic text-center">
            Sitting still is where much of the effect shows up: lower cortisol, slower pulse, calmer blood pressure.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-3xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light pt-4" id="how-to-practice">
          How to Practice Shinrin-Yoku: A Step-by-Step Guide
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          The beauty of Shinrin-Yoku is its simplicity. You don't need special training, expensive equipment, or perfect physical fitness. Here's how to practice forest bathing effectively:
        </p>

        <!-- Step 1 -->
        <div class="rounded-xl p-6 my-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-2xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light mb-4" id="step-1">
            Step 1: Choose Your Forest Location
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-text-main-light mb-4">
            You don't need a pristine wilderness area. Any space with trees will work:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 ml-4 mb-4">
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Local parks with wooded areas</li>
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Nature preserves or green spaces</li>
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Botanical gardens with tree canopy</li>
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Trail systems with gentle, accessible paths</li>
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Even tree-lined neighborhoods can offer benefits</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-sm text-accent-dark italic">The ideal location has sufficient tree coverage, safe paths, minimal urban noise, and places to sit and rest.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 2 -->
        <div class="rounded-xl p-6 my-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-2xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light mb-4" id="step-2">
            Step 2: Plan for 2-3 Hours
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-text-main-light mb-4">
            Research suggests that 2-3 hours is optimal for experiencing the full therapeutic effects. This doesn't mean continuous walking. It includes time for sitting, observing, and simply being present.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-accent-dark italic">If 2-3 hours seems daunting, start with 20-30 minutes and gradually increase your time.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 3 -->
        <div class="rounded-xl p-6 my-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-2xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light mb-4" id="step-3">
            Step 3: Leave Technology Behind
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-text-main-light mb-4">
            To fully engage with nature, minimize digital distractions:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 ml-4">
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Put your phone on silent or airplane mode</li>
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Avoid listening to music or podcasts</li>
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Skip the fitness tracker for this activity</li>
            <li class="text-base text-text-main-light">• Leave the camera behind (or only use it sparingly)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 4 -->
        <div class="rounded-xl p-6 my-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-2xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light mb-4" id="step-4">
            Step 4: Walk Slowly and Aimlessly
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-text-main-light mb-4">
            This isn't exercise. There's no need to maintain a brisk pace or cover distance. Walk slowly enough that you can notice details around you. Some practitioners recommend covering less than half a mile in an hour.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base text-text-main-light">
            Let yourself wander without a specific route. The Japanese call this "forest rambling," allowing curiosity and intuition to guide your path.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 5 - Five Senses -->
        <div class="rounded-xl p-6 my-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-2xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light mb-4" id="step-5">
            Step 5: Engage All Five Senses
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-text-main-light mb-4">
            This is the heart of Shinrin-Yoku. Consciously use each sense to connect with your surroundings:
          </p>
          <figure class="my-8">
            <div class="w-full max-w-lg mx-auto aspect-[3/2] rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg">
              <img
                src={fiveSensesImage.src}
                alt="Illustration of the five senses in a forest setting: sight, sound, smell, touch and taste linked to leaves, birds, pine, bark and fresh air"
                class="w-full h-full object-cover"
              />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-text-muted-light mt-3 italic text-center">
              The five doorways into the practice. Use one at a time rather than trying to track all five at once.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <div class="grid gap-4">
            <div>
              <strong class="text-text-main-light">Sight:</strong>
              <span class="text-text-muted-light"> Notice patterns of light and shadow, different shades of green, texture of bark, movement of leaves.</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-text-main-light">Sound:</strong>
              <span class="text-text-muted-light"> Listen to birdsong, rustling leaves, flowing water, wind through branches, your own footsteps.</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-text-main-light">Smell:</strong>
              <span class="text-text-muted-light"> Breathe deeply and notice pine needles, damp earth, wild flowers, fresh air.</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-text-main-light">Touch:</strong>
              <span class="text-text-muted-light"> Feel the texture of tree bark, moss, leaves, and stones. Notice temperature differences.</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-text-main-light">Taste:</strong>
              <span class="text-text-muted-light"> "Taste" the fresh air and notice how it feels different from city air.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Steps 6-8 -->
        <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
          <div class="border-l-4 border-accent-dark pl-6 py-2">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-text-main-light mb-2" id="step-6">Step 6: Practice Mindful Presence</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Bring your attention fully to the present moment. When your mind wanders to worries or distractions, gently bring focus back to your sensory experience. You might silently note: "I'm seeing sunlight through leaves," "I'm hearing a woodpecker."</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-l-4 border-accent-dark pl-6 py-2">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-text-main-light mb-2" id="step-7">Step 7: Take Breaks to Sit and Observe</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Find spots to sit for 10-15 minutes at a time. Watch the forest without analyzing, notice how different stillness feels, practice deep breathing, and allow yourself to simply be. These rest periods are when many physiological benefits become most pronounced.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-l-4 border-accent-dark pl-6 py-2">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-text-main-light mb-2" id="step-8">Step 8: Finish with Gratitude</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light">Before leaving, take a moment to thank the forest, reflect on what you noticed, set an intention to return, and notice how you feel compared to when you arrived. This closing ritual reinforces the positive effects.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- What to Bring Section -->
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-3xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light pt-4" id="what-to-bring">
          What to Bring for Shinrin-Yoku
        </h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">
              Essential Items
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2">
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Comfortable, weather-appropriate clothing</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Supportive walking shoes with good traction</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Water bottle to stay hydrated</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Light snack if you have blood sugar concerns</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Cell phone for emergencies (kept on silent)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-3">
              Optional Helpful Items
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2">
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Small folding seat or cushion</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Light rain jacket</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Sun hat and sunscreen</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Insect repellent during bug season</li>
              <li class="text-base text-text-muted-light">• Small notebook for reflections</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="my-8">
          <div class="w-full aspect-[3/2] rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg">
            <img
              src={whatToBringImage.src}
              alt="Flat lay of forest bathing essentials: walking shoes, water bottle, light rain jacket, sun hat, folding seat and a small notebook"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-text-muted-light mt-3 italic text-center">
            Nothing here is required. Comfortable shoes, water and something to sit on cover almost every outing.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Tips for Limited Mobility -->
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-3xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light pt-4" id="limited-mobility">
          Tips for Seniors with Limited Mobility
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-text-main-light font-normal">
          Forest bathing is remarkably accessible, even for people with physical limitations:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-2xl p-8 my-6">
          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-2">Walking Challenges</h4>
              <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm text-text-muted-light">
                <li>• Choose paved or well-maintained trails</li>
                <li>• Use walking or trekking poles</li>
                <li>• Consider a rollator with a seat</li>
                <li>• Visit wheelchair-accessible trails</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-2">Balance Concerns</h4>
              <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm text-text-muted-light">
                <li>• Stay on wider trails</li>
                <li>• Have a companion walk with you</li>
                <li>• Choose flatter terrain</li>
                <li>• Practice seated on a bench</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-2">Fatigue Issues</h4>
              <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm text-text-muted-light">
                <li>• Start with 20-minute sessions</li>
                <li>• Drive to trailhead, walk short loops</li>
                <li>• Choose locations with restrooms</li>
                <li>• Go during mild weather</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light mt-4 italic text-center">
            Remember: Even sitting in a forest for 30 minutes provides measurable health benefits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="my-8">
          <div class="w-full aspect-[3/2] rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg">
            <img
              src={accessibleTrailImage.src}
              alt="Senior walking slowly along a wide, level, well-surfaced forest trail using a rollator with a seat"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-text-muted-light mt-3 italic text-center">
            A paved loop with benches counts. Even thirty minutes seated among trees produces measurable changes.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Best Times Section -->
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.5rem] font-medium lg:text-3xl lg:font-medium text-text-main-light pt-4" id="best-times">
          Best Times and Seasons for Forest Bathing
        </h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-2">Spring</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-text-muted-light">Nature's renewal, mild temperatures, blooming wildflowers, higher phytoncide levels. Can be muddy.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-2">Summer</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-text-muted-light">Lush canopy provides shade, peak phytoncide production, longer days. Go early morning or evening.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-2">Fall</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-text-muted-light">Spectacular colors, comfortable temperatures, earthy scents, fewer crowds than summer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-text-main-light mb-2">Winter</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-text-muted-light">Unique beauty and stillness, evergreens maintain benefits, fewer crowds. Requires warm layers.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-base text-text-muted-light italic">
          <strong>Optimal Time of Day:</strong> Morning sessions (before 11 AM) provide cooler temperatures, higher phytoncide concentrations, and quieter trails.
        </p>

        <figure class="my-8">
          <div class="w-full aspect-[3/2] rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg">
            <img
              src={fourSeasonsImage.src}
              alt="The same forest path shown across the four seasons: spring blossom, summer canopy, autumn colour and winter snow"
